Z||000000||Feenx||12-12-2000||07:43 PM||||Hi All<BR>does anyone know if you can test a fuel injecter while it is out of the car.<BR>it's from a 86 2.5 <BR>I read some where that it needs between <BR>(4 to 6.5 volts at 4 amps)but not sure.<BR>any help would be great<BR><P>------------------<BR>86 Sport Coupe (Red)<BR>2.5l Auto||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||12-12-2000||07:59 PM||||The only thing that you can check by yourself with the injector out is the resistance, and the output.  You can measure resistance by using an ohm-meter on the two terminals.  I don't know what the specs are, but that's how you check resistance.  To measure output, you have to bypass the normal computer output first.  You have to run a constantly + wire to the fuel pump for that.  Then you pump fuel into a container and measure the amount of fuel you get in a certain amount of time (10/30/60 seconds).  I again don't know these specs for your application.  NOTE!!! Before you do a output check, unplug all of the other injectors first so that you don't go pumping raw fuel into the cylinders without the car running!  And be careful with fuel at all times...running wires around and using an open fuel container can be very dangerous.  Hope this helps some, I'm sure others will have more to add/correct <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-12-2000||08:01 PM||||You can't reliably test it the way it sounds like you are trying to do. The driver circuit required is more than a simple switch. It regulates the current as well as switching power. If you hook it up directly to a power supply you'll likely fry the coil.<P>What is it you think is wrong with it?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||12-12-2000||08:13 PM||||Oh yeah, theogre is right, I forgot to mention that.  You can't just put voltage to the injector.  You have to let it go through the computer.  The computer regulates the cycles it runs (open/closed duration) and the amount of power going to it.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
